# description : This .audit is designed against the CIS Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9 STIG Benchmark v1.0.0
#
#<ui_metadata>
#<display_name>CIS Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9 STIG v1.0.0 CAT I</display_name>
#<spec>
#  <type>CIS</type>
#  <name>Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9 STIG</name>
#  <profile>CAT I</profile>
#  <version>1.0.0</version>
#  <link>https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22008</link>
#</spec>
#<labels>agent,rhel_linux_9</labels>
#<benchmark_refs>800-53,800-53r5,CAT,CCI,LEVEL,Rule-ID,STIG-ID,Vuln-ID</benchmark_refs>
#<variables>
#  <variable>
#    <name>PLATFORM_VERSION</name>
#    <default>9</default>
#    <description>Linux Distribution Version</description>
#    <info>Linux Distribution Version</info>
#    <value_type>STRING</value_type>
#  </variable>
#</variables>
#</ui_metadata>

<check_type:"Unix">

<if>
  <condition type:"AND">
    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description : "Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9 is installed"
      file        : "/etc/system-release"
      regex       : "^[\\s]*Red Hat Enterprise Linux.*release"
      expect      : "^[\\s]*Red Hat Enterprise Linux.*release @PLATFORM_VERSION@"
    </custom_item>
  </condition>

  <then>
    <report type:"PASSED">
      description : "CIS_Red_Hat_Enterprise_Linux_9_STIG_v1.0.0_CAT_I.audit from CIS Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9 STIG v1.0.0"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22008"
      show_output : YES
    </report>

    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "1.105 RHEL-09-231190"
      info        : "RHEL 9 local disk partitions must implement cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized disclosure or modification of all information that requires at rest protection.

GROUP ID: V-257879
RULE ID: SV-257879r1045454

RHEL 9 systems handling data requiring \"data at rest\" protections must employ cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized disclosure and modification of the information at rest.

Selection of a cryptographic mechanism is based on the need to protect the integrity of organizational information. The strength of the mechanism is commensurate with the security category and/or classification of the information. Organizations have the flexibility to either encrypt all information on storage devices (i.e., full disk encryption) or encrypt specific data structures (e.g., files, records, or fields).

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000405-GPOS-00184, SRG-OS-000185-GPOS-00079, SRG-OS-000404-GPOS-00183

NOTE: Nessus has provided the target output to assist in reviewing the benchmark to ensure target compliance."
      solution    : "Configure RHEL 9 to prevent unauthorized modification of all information at rest by using disk encryption.

Encrypting a partition in an already installed system is more difficult, because existing partitions will need to be resized and changed.

To encrypt an entire partition, dedicate a partition for encryption in the partition layout."
      reference   : "800-171|3.13.16,800-171r3|03.13.08,800-53|SC-28,800-53|SC-28(1),800-53r5|SC-28,800-53r5|SC-28(1),CAT|I,CCI|CCI-001199,CCI|CCI-002475,CCI|CCI-002476,CN-L3|8.1.4.7(b),CN-L3|8.1.4.8(b),CSF|PR.DS-1,CSF2.0|PR.DS-01,GDPR|32.1.a,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(iv),HIPAA|164.312(e)(2)(ii),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.10,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.33,ITSG-33|SC-28,ITSG-33|SC-28a.,ITSG-33|SC-28(1),LEVEL|1M,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|3.4,PCI-DSSv4.0|3.3.2,PCI-DSSv4.0|3.5.1,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|6.2,Rule-ID|SV-257879r1045454_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-09-231190,TBA-FIISB|28.1,Vuln-ID|V-257879"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22008"
      cmd         : "/sbin/blkid"
      expect      : "^Manual Review Required$"
      severity    : MEDIUM
    </custom_item>

    <if>
      <condition type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description : "root, admin, or administrator"
          file        : "/etc/grub2.cfg"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*set[\\s]*superusers[\\s]*"
          expect      : "^[\\s]*set[\\s]*superusers[\\s]*=[\\s]*\"(?i)(root|admin|administrator)(?-i)\"$"
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK_NOT
          description : "1.13 RHEL-09-212020"
          info        : "RHEL 9 must require a unique superusers name upon booting into single-user and maintenance modes.

GROUP ID: V-257789
RULE ID: SV-257789r1069356

Having a nondefault grub superuser username makes password-guessing attacks less effective."
          solution    : "Configure RHEL 9 to have a unique username for the grub superuser account.

Edit the \"/etc/grub.d/01_users\" file and add or modify the following lines with a nondefault username for the superuser account:

set superusers=\"<accountname>\"
export superusers

Once the superuser account has been added, update the grub.cfg file by running:

Regenerate the GRUB configuration:

$ sudo grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg

Reboot the system:

$ sudo reboot"
          reference   : "800-171|3.1.1,800-171r3|03.01.02,800-53|AC-3,800-53r5|AC-3,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000213,CN-L3|8.1.4.2(f),CN-L3|8.1.4.11(b),CN-L3|8.1.10.2(c),CN-L3|8.5.3.1,CN-L3|8.5.4.1(a),CSF|PR.AC-4,CSF|PR.PT-3,CSF2.0|PR.AA-05,CSF2.0|PR.DS-10,CSF2.0|PR.IR-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.15,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.33,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.3,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.18,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.20,ISO/IEC-27001|A.9.4.1,ISO/IEC-27001|A.9.4.5,ITSG-33|AC-3,LEVEL|1M,NESA|T4.2.1,NESA|T5.4.4,NESA|T5.4.5,NESA|T5.5.4,NESA|T5.6.1,NESA|T7.5.2,NESA|T7.5.3,NIAv2|AM3,NIAv2|SS29,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-257789r1069356_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-09-212020,TBA-FIISB|31.1,Vuln-ID|V-257789"
          see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22008"
          file        : "/etc/grub2.cfg"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*set[\\s]*superusers[\\s]*"
          expect      : "^[\\s]*set[\\s]*superusers[\\s]*=[\\s]*\"(?i)(root|admin|administrator)(?-i)\"$"
        </custom_item>
      </then>

      <else>
        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description : "1.13 RHEL-09-212020"
          info        : "RHEL 9 must require a unique superusers name upon booting into single-user and maintenance modes.

GROUP ID: V-257789
RULE ID: SV-257789r1069356

Having a nondefault grub superuser username makes password-guessing attacks less effective.

NOTE: Nessus has provided the target output to assist in reviewing the benchmark to ensure target compliance."
          solution    : "Configure RHEL 9 to have a unique username for the grub superuser account.

Edit the \"/etc/grub.d/01_users\" file and add or modify the following lines with a nondefault username for the superuser account:

set superusers=\"<accountname>\"
export superusers

Once the superuser account has been added, update the grub.cfg file by running:

Regenerate the GRUB configuration:

$ sudo grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg

Reboot the system:

$ sudo reboot"
          reference   : "800-171|3.1.1,800-171r3|03.01.02,800-53|AC-3,800-53r5|AC-3,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000213,CN-L3|8.1.4.2(f),CN-L3|8.1.4.11(b),CN-L3|8.1.10.2(c),CN-L3|8.5.3.1,CN-L3|8.5.4.1(a),CSF|PR.AC-4,CSF|PR.PT-3,CSF2.0|PR.AA-05,CSF2.0|PR.DS-10,CSF2.0|PR.IR-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.15,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.33,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.3,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.18,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.20,ISO/IEC-27001|A.9.4.1,ISO/IEC-27001|A.9.4.5,ITSG-33|AC-3,LEVEL|1M,NESA|T4.2.1,NESA|T5.4.4,NESA|T5.4.5,NESA|T5.5.4,NESA|T5.6.1,NESA|T7.5.2,NESA|T7.5.3,NIAv2|AM3,NIAv2|SS29,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-257789r1069356_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-09-212020,TBA-FIISB|31.1,Vuln-ID|V-257789"
          see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22008"
          file        : "/etc/grub2.cfg"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*set[\\s]*superusers[\\s]*"
          expect      : "^Manual Review Required$"
          severity    : MEDIUM
        </custom_item>
      </else>
    </if>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FIND_CMD
      description : "1.180 RHEL-09-252070"
      info        : "There must be no shosts.equiv files on RHEL 9.

GROUP ID: V-257955
RULE ID: SV-257955r991589

The shosts.equiv files are used to configure host-based authentication for the system via SSH. Host-based authentication is not sufficient for preventing unauthorized access to the system, as it does not require interactive identification and authentication of a connection request, or for the use of two-factor authentication."
      solution    : "Remove any found \"shosts.equiv\" files from the system.

$ sudo rm /[path]/[to]/[file]/shosts.equiv"
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|1M,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-257955r991589_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-09-252070,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-257955"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22008"
      not_expect  : ".+"
      find_name   : "shosts.equiv"
      find_type   : "f"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FIND_CMD
      description : "1.181 RHEL-09-252075"
      info        : "There must be no .shosts files on RHEL 9.

GROUP ID: V-257956
RULE ID: SV-257956r991589

The .shosts files are used to configure host-based authentication for individual users or the system via SSH. Host-based authentication is not sufficient for preventing unauthorized access to the system, as it does not require interactive identification and authentication of a connection request, or for the use of two-factor authentication."
      solution    : "Remove any found \".shosts\" files from the system.

$ sudo rm /[path]/[to]/[file]/.shosts"
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|1M,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-257956r991589_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-09-252075,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-257956"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22008"
      not_expect  : ".+"
      find_name   : ".shosts"
      find_type   : "f"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description : "1.2 RHEL-09-211010"
      info        : "RHEL 9 must be a vendor-supported release.

GROUP ID: V-257777
RULE ID: SV-257777r991589

An operating system release is considered \"supported\" if the vendor continues to provide security patches for the product. With an unsupported release, it will not be possible to resolve security issues discovered in the system software.

Red Hat offers the Extended Update Support (EUS) add-on to a Red Hat Enterprise Linux subscription, for a fee, for those customers who wish to standardize on a specific minor release for an extended period."
      solution    : "Upgrade to a supported version of RHEL 9."
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|1M,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-257777r991589_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-09-211010,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-257777"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22008"
      file        : "/etc/redhat-release"
      regex       : "^[\\s]*Red[\\s]+Hat[\\s]+Enterprise[\\s]+Linux"
      expect      : "^[\\s]*Red[\\s]+Hat[\\s]+Enterprise[\\s]+Linux[\\s]+release[\\s]+9\\.[0-9]+.*$"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "1.209 RHEL-09-255040"
      info        : "RHEL 9 SSHD must not allow blank passwords.

GROUP ID: V-257984
RULE ID: SV-257984r1045026

If an account has an empty password, anyone could log on and run commands with the privileges of that account. Accounts with empty passwords should never be used in operational environments.

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000106-GPOS-00053, SRG-OS-000480-GPOS-00229, SRG-OS-000480-GPOS-00227"
      solution    : "To configure the system to prevent SSH users from logging on with blank passwords edit the following line in \"/etc/ssh/sshd_config\" or in a file in \"/etc/ssh/sshd_config.d\":

PermitEmptyPasswords no

Restart the SSH daemon for the settings to take effect:

$ sudo systemctl restart sshd.service"
      reference   : "800-171|3.5.3,800-171r3|03.05.03,800-53|IA-2(2),800-53r5|IA-2(2),CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000766,CN-L3|7.1.3.1(a),CN-L3|7.1.3.1(e),CN-L3|8.1.4.1(a),CN-L3|8.1.4.2(a),CN-L3|8.5.4.1(a),CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(d),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.16,ITSG-33|IA-2(2),LEVEL|1A,NESA|T5.4.2,NIAv2|AM2,NIAv2|AM8,NIAv2|AM14b,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-257984r1045026_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-09-255040,SWIFT-CSCv1|1.2,TBA-FIISB|35.1,TBA-FIISB|36.1,Vuln-ID|V-257984"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22008"
      cmd         : "/sbin/sshd -T | /bin/grep -i PermitEmptyPasswords"
      expect      : "(?i)[\\s]*PermitEmptyPasswords[\\s]+\"?no\"?[\\s]*$"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "1.211 RHEL-09-255050"
      info        : "RHEL 9 must enable the Pluggable Authentication Module (PAM) interface for SSHD.

GROUP ID: V-257986
RULE ID: SV-257986r1045030

When UsePAM is set to \"yes\", PAM runs through account and session types properly. This is important when restricted access to services based off of IP, time, or other factors of the account is needed. Additionally, this ensures users can inherit certain environment variables on login or disallow access to the server."
      solution    : "Configure the RHEL 9 SSHD to use the UsePAM interface by adding or modifying the following line in \"/etc/ssh/sshd_config\" or in a file in \"/etc/ssh/sshd_config.d\".

UsePAM yes

Restart the SSH daemon for the settings to take effect:

$ sudo systemctl restart sshd.service"
      reference   : "800-171|3.7.5,800-171r3|03.07.05b.,800-53|MA-4c.,800-53r5|MA-4c.,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000877,CSF|PR.MA-2,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|MA-4c.,LEVEL|1A,NESA|T2.3.4,NESA|T5.4.4,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,Rule-ID|SV-257986r1045030_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-09-255050,TBA-FIISB|45.2.3,Vuln-ID|V-257986"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22008"
      cmd         : "/sbin/sshd -T | /bin/grep -i UsePAM"
      expect      : "(?i)[\\s]*UsePAM[\\s]+\"?yes\"?[\\s]*$"
    </custom_item>

    <if>
      <condition type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type        : RPM_CHECK
          description : "Check GDM installed"
          rpm         : "gdm-0.0.0-0"
          operator    : "gt"
          required    : YES
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description : "1.243 RHEL-09-271040"
          info        : "RHEL 9 must not allow unattended or automatic logon via the graphical user interface.

GROUP ID: V-258018
RULE ID: SV-258018r1045090

Failure to restrict system access to authenticated users negatively impacts operating system security."
          solution    : "Configure the GNOME desktop display manager to disable automatic login.

Set AutomaticLoginEnable to false in the [daemon] section in /etc/gdm/custom.conf. For example:

[daemon]
AutomaticLoginEnable=false"
          reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|1A,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-258018r1045090_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-09-271040,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-258018"
          see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22008"
          file        : "/etc/gdm/custom.conf"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*(?i)AutomaticLoginEnable[\\s]*=[\\s]*"
          expect      : "^[\\s]*(?i)AutomaticLoginEnable[\\s]*=[\\s]*false[\\s]*$"
        </custom_item>
      </then>

      <else>
        <report type:"PASSED">
          description : "1.243 RHEL-09-271040"
          info        : "RHEL 9 must not allow unattended or automatic logon via the graphical user interface.

GROUP ID: V-258018
RULE ID: SV-258018r1045090

Failure to restrict system access to authenticated users negatively impacts operating system security."
          solution    : "Configure the GNOME desktop display manager to disable automatic login.

Set AutomaticLoginEnable to false in the [daemon] section in /etc/gdm/custom.conf. For example:

[daemon]
AutomaticLoginEnable=false"
          reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|1A,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-258018r1045090_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-09-271040,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-258018"
          see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22008"
        </report>
      </else>
    </if>

    <item>
      description : "1.284 RHEL-09-411100"
      info        : "The root account must be the only account having unrestricted access to RHEL 9 system.

GROUP ID: V-258059
RULE ID: SV-258059r991589

An account has root authority if it has a user identifier (UID) of \"0\". Multiple accounts with a UID of \"0\" afford more opportunity for potential intruders to guess a password for a privileged account. Proper configuration of sudo is recommended to afford multiple system administrators access to root privileges in an accountable manner."
      solution    : "Change the UID of any account on the system, other than root, that has a UID of \"0\".

If the account is associated with system commands or applications, the UID should be changed to one greater than \"0\" but less than \"1000\". Otherwise, assign a UID of greater than \"1000\" that has not already been assigned."
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|1A,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-258059r991589_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-09-411100,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-258059"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/22008"
      name        : "passwd_zero_uid"
    </item>

    <if>
      <condition auto:"FAILED" type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type        : CMD_EXEC
          description : "SELinux enabled"
          cmd         : "/sbin/getenforce"
          expect      : "^Enforcing$"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description : "SELINUX enforcing config file"
          file        : "/etc/selinux/config"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*(?i)SELINUX(?-i)[\\s]*=[\\s]*"
          expect      : "^[\\s]*(?i)SELINUX(?-i)[\\s]*=[\\s]*enforcing[\\s]*$"
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <report type:"PASSED">
          description : "1.298 RHEL-09-431010"
          info        : "RHEL 9 must use a Linux Security Module configured to enforce limits on system services.

GROUP ID: V-258078
RULE ID: SV-258078r958944

Without verification of the security functions, security functions may not operate correctly and the failure may go unnoticed. Security function is defined as the hardware, software, and/or firmware of the information system responsible for enforcing the system security policy and supporting the isolation of code and data on which the protection is based. Security functionality includes, but is not limited to, establishing system accounts, configuring access authorizations (i.e., permissions, privileges), setting events to be audited, and setting intrusion detection parameters.

This requirement applies to operating systems performing security function verification/testing and/or systems and environments that require this functionality.

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000445-GPOS-00199, SRG-OS-000134-GPOS-00068"
          solution    : "Configure RHEL 9 to verify correct operation of security functions.

Edit the file \"/etc/selinux/config\" and add or modify the following line:

SELINUX=enforcing

A reboot is required for the changes to take effect."
